BE-300 2.4.18: skip invalidate_device() in mtdblock_release.

invalidate_device(inode->i_rdev, 1) hangs forever when called from
mount_root's blkdev_put on the JFFS2 root mtdblock3.  It walks the
page cache for the block device looking for dirty buffers to sync,
finds none (mtdblock has no buffer-cache pages of its own — JFFS2
talks to the MTD layer directly via mtdblock_readsect/writesect),
but the sync path still tries to acquire the mtdblock cache_sem
that this release function is itself about to acquire below at
`down(&mtdblk->cache_sem)`.  The result is that mtdblock_release
sleeps inside invalidate_device waiting for a buffer-IO completion
that never fires, while holding nothing — but the rest of the
mount-path machinery (BKL, lock_super, etc.) blocks on the original
blkdev_put, leading to the boot-time hang documented in the earlier
2.4 Opie work.

The cache state at this release point is empty for a fresh RO mount,
so skipping the invalidate is safe.  Comment block left in place to
document the rationale for future readers.

This was previously carried as an inline edit in the extracted
linux-2.4.18/ tree; capturing it as a numbered patch so a fresh
extract + apply_patch_series.sh reproduces the same state.

--- a/drivers/mtd/mtdblock.c
+++ b/drivers/mtd/mtdblock.c
@@ -371,7 +371,12 @@ static release_t mtdblock_release(struct inode *inode, struct file *file)
 	if (inode == NULL)
 		release_return(-ENODEV);

-	invalidate_device(inode->i_rdev, 1);
+	/* invalidate_device(inode->i_rdev, 1) hangs forever when called from
+	 * mount_root's blkdev_put on the JFFS2 root mtdblock3 — it tries to
+	 * sync-flush a non-existent page cache through the MTD layer and
+	 * deadlocks against the cache_sem we're about to acquire below.
+	 * The mount-side cache is empty for a fresh RO mount, so just skip
+	 * the invalidate.  See BE300_2_4 Opie boot path notes. */

 	dev = MINOR(inode->i_rdev);
 	mtdblk = mtdblks[dev];
